    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 348.153, 538.244, 403.851 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "FIG. 1: (upper) Energies projected to positive parity states, Jπ = 0+ and 2+ states, (middle) the triaxiality γ for protons and neutrons, and (lower) the H.O. values relative to lowest allowed states are plotted as functions of the quadrupole deformation parameter β of the total system."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 36,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 209.279, 538.287, 326.93 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "performed for the Jπ = 2+ states. Those energy curves have two local minima at β ∼ 0.2 and 0.5, which are labeled GS and SD minima, respectively, because the GCM results show that these minima correspond to the GS and the SD states, respectively, as explained later. In the energy curves projected to positive parity states, the energy gap between the GS and SD minima is approximately 6.7 MeV, while the gap decreases to 2.5 MeV after the AMP to Jπ = 0+ states."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
